{
  "correction_rules": [
    "General level bias: model is systematically too low versus realized prices in rising regimes (typical underprediction ~+1 to +3 EUR across much of the 1–25 day horizon). Apply an upward level adjustment when the last 10–15 days show a positive trend or when spot is above the 20–30d mean.",
    "Lag in uptrends: model reacts too slowly to bullish breaks (misses step-ups around days 5–18 in examples). If a breakout occurs (e.g., price > prior 10d high), shift the forecast path upward quickly rather than gradually (front-load the adjustment into days 1–10).",
    "Horizon-dependent sign flip: near the far end of the 30d horizon the model often becomes too high (overshoots) while reality mean-reverts/rolls over (e.g., examples 1, 4, 5 last ~5–10 days). Dampen late-horizon drift by pulling days 20–30 toward the current spot/30d mean.",
    "Overreaction to late spikes in the forecast: when the model projects sharp late-horizon rallies (e.g., >+3 EUR over 5–10 days), realized outcomes tend to be flatter or reversing. Cap late-horizon acceleration and impose stronger mean reversion after large projected gains.",
    "Asymmetry: model captures down moves better than up moves; correction should be conditional—apply stronger upward correction in uptrends than downward correction in downtrends.",
    "Volatility/dispersion underestimation during rallies: realized paths show higher amplitude than forecasts in the middle horizon (days ~7–20). Increase forecast variance (wider band) or add a convex uplift during sustained momentum periods."
  ]
}
